# React hooks

## Custom hooks

- Name `useX`, one hook per file, path `src/hooks/useX.ts` (use `.tsx` only if the file contains JSX).
- Call hooks only at the top level of a component or custom hook — never in conditions, loops, or callbacks.
- Extract data loading, session flow, and derived app state into hooks. Screens should mostly call hooks and pass results to views.
- Hooks return data + callbacks, **never JSX**.
- Keep domain/scheduling logic in `src/services/`; hooks orchestrate React state around those services.

## Built-in hooks

- `useState` — UI and hook-local state only. Do not mirror server/DB state in many places; reload via a single hook (see `useAppData`).
- `useEffect` — synchronize with external systems (DB, subscriptions). Always return cleanup when needed. List every used value in the dependency array.
- `useMemo` / `useCallback` — only when the value is expensive or a child/effect depends on referential equality. Do not wrap everything.
- `useRef` — mutable values that must not trigger render (guards, DOM nodes). Do not use refs as a state store.

## Context

- Pair provider + consumer hook in the same file. The consumer **throws** if used outside the provider.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — effect without cleanup / deps; logic stuck in the Screen
useEffect(() => {
  const t = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  if (ready) load();
});

// ✅ GOOD — hook owns lifecycle; Screen only consumes
export function useWordDetail(wordId: string) {
  const [word, setWord] = useState<Word | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    void db.words.get(wordId).then((row) => {
      if (!cancelled) setWord(row ?? null);
    });
    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [wordId]);
  return { word };
}
```
